From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: keep restoring the endpoint until it answers

The 051 log finally shows what the recovery has been fighting: it is not
one event, it is a window.  The endpoint is still re-initialising its PCIe
core when the patch hands the chip back, and the restore written into that
window is simply lost.

Nothing on the host side notices, because the write does stick as far as
the config space is concerned - PCI_COMMAND reads back 0x0006 - while the
memory window itself stays shut and every MMIO read falls through to DRAM:

  diag: config cleared (cmd=0x0000), bar0=0x20000000 lpctl=0xe62140b6 glo=0x40a05c74
  diag: fw owned after 20 ms (lpctl=0x53c8363f)
  diag: driver own ret=0 lpctl=0xad17aefa
  diag rx[pre-rebuild]: base=0x8ecb5aea cnt=0x7f1d14b5 cidx=0xd27a0511 didx=0x47c08e4a sw=1/2

Those are DRAM contents, not registers.  The give-away is the event ring
base: it is programmed once and never moves by itself, yet it printed
0x8ecb5aea and a completely different value on the next sample.  Everything
after that point - the ownership pulse, the ring rebuild, the semaphore
release - ran against a chip that was not listening, which is why the
replies kept "not arriving" and why the run behaved differently from the
previous one with the same code.

Restore the configuration in a loop instead, and only believe it once the
chip answers like a chip: LPCTL carries nothing but the ownership bits, and
the command ring has to hand back the base and size that were just
programmed into it.  Three good answers in a row are required before the
rings are rebuilt, and a bad ring readback after the rebuild starts the
whole attempt over.

While here, stop forcing ownership the instant the response arrives.  A
started patch takes the chip over and then gives it back on its own, so
wait up to two seconds for that release and only take it by force when it
never comes; the old code grabbed it in the same millisecond and could not
tell the two cases apart.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
